# Article: Project

This project is `generic-scraper`: a reusable, configuration-driven web
scraping library and CLI written in Python. The point of the project is that a
user describes a target site in a spec file and gets structured records out,
without writing Python.

## Swarm

Six roles, all on the `claude` backend (Claude Code), signed in to the user's
Claude account. Do not switch backends or edit `swarmforge/swarmforge.conf`.

## Layout

```
swarmforge/              role prompts, constitution, swarm config
.swarmforge/             swarm state -- never edit by hand
.worktrees/<role>/       one git worktree per role -- stay in your own
src/generic_scraper/     library code
tests/                   unit tests, mirroring the src layout
tests/acceptance/        end-to-end tests against local fixtures
fixtures/                saved HTML/JSON pages the tests scrape
docs/                    usage documentation
```

## Product intent

A scraper you configure rather than rewrite. Given a spec -- base URL,
pagination rule, per-field selectors, output shape -- the tool produces
structured records.

Build in roughly this order:

1. Spec loading and validation. A malformed spec fails fast with a message that
   names the offending key and what was expected.
2. A fetch layer over `requests`: timeouts, retry with exponential backoff on
   5xx and connection errors, and a pluggable transport so tests inject a fake
   instead of touching the network.
3. Field extraction by CSS selector (BeautifulSoup + lxml), with optional type
   coercion (`str`, `int`, `float`, `date`) and a per-field `required` flag.
4. Pagination strategies: `next-link` (follow a selector), `page-number`
   (template a query param until empty), and `none`.
5. Output writers: JSON Lines and CSV.
6. A CLI: `scrape <spec.yaml> -o out.jsonl`, with `--limit`, `--dry-run`, and
   sensible exit codes.
7. Politeness, on by default: per-domain rate limiting and robots.txt respect.

Out of scope unless the user explicitly asks: JavaScript rendering or headless
browsers, distributed crawling, proxy rotation, captcha handling.

## Acceptance specs (Gherkin)

This project is spec-first. Hand-written `.feature` files are the source of
truth for behavior and they arrive in the repo before the swarm starts.

- Feature files live in `features/`, one file per capability
  (`features/spec_loading.feature`, `features/pagination.feature`, ...).
- Step definitions live in `tests/acceptance/steps/`, run by `pytest-bdd` so
  there is exactly one test runner for the whole project. `pytest` alone runs
  everything.
- Fixtures the scenarios scrape live in `fixtures/`, committed.

The specifier does **not** invent features that already exist in `features/`.
Its job on an existing file is to review it, normalize it to the project's
Gherkin conventions, prune example-table columns that do not change behavior,
consolidate repeated setup into `Background`, flag anything ambiguous or
untestable back to the user, and only then cut a slice and hand off. New
features it writes follow the same conventions and land in the same directory.

The coder implements against a scenario; nobody edits a `.feature` file to make
a failing scenario pass. If a scenario is wrong, hand it back to the specifier
and say why.

## Engineering constraints

- Python 3.11+. Standard library plus `requests`, `beautifulsoup4`, `lxml`,
  `PyYAML`, `pytest`. Adding any other dependency needs the architect's
  agreement first.
- No network in tests. Ever. Tests run against committed fixtures or an injected
  fake transport. A test that reaches the live internet is a defect, and the
  hardener should kill it.
- Type hints on every public function. `ruff` clean, `mypy src` clean.
- Public behavior is documented in `docs/` in the same slice that ships it.

## Guardrails

This is a general-purpose tool, not an evasion tool. Do not implement, and do
not accept a handoff asking for: bypassing captchas or login walls, spoofing to
defeat bot detection, disabling robots.txt by default, or harvesting personal
data. If a handoff asks for one of these, refuse it, say why in a note back to
the specifier, and stop rather than improvising a variant.

## Communication

Handoffs are short and direct: what changed, what remains, what you need from
the next role. No verification transcripts. No commentary about your own
process.

## Governance

Each role owns its own prompt and workflow. Do not edit another role's prompt,
`swarmforge.conf`, or the constitution without explicit authorization from the
user.
